1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,499
A customer wants cheese for a tasting:
12 guests in Pennsylvania, a $150 budget,

2
00:00:06,499 --> 00:00:11,861
no nuts, a French cheese,
and one with a funk rating of 4 or more.

3
00:00:12,181 --> 00:00:14,976
Here are two answers
from the same model and tools.

4
00:00:15,217 --> 00:00:20,380
The first proposes 1,050 grams
for 5,600 cents.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,129
The second, 1,200 grams for 6,400 cents,

6
00:00:24,129 --> 00:00:28,477
within a budget with the comma
in the wrong place:

7
00:00:28,477 --> 00:00:30,129
a malformed number.

8
00:00:30,367 --> 00:00:33,183
Which of these would you
let charge a customer's card?

9
00:00:33,503 --> 00:00:35,337
And what would have to be true first?

10
00:00:36,583 --> 00:00:38,354
The second run printed more.

11
00:00:38,674 --> 00:00:41,549
Code checked the cart itself:

12
00:00:41,549 --> 00:00:47,100
1,200 grams, $64, shop rules satisfied,
no order placed.

13
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:50,706
The cart was valid;
only the sentence was garbled.

14
00:00:51,026 --> 00:00:53,714
Nothing checked the first
answer's final cart.

15
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:58,921
The course notebook says it in one line:
the model proposes; the harness decides.

16
00:00:59,241 --> 00:01:01,807
The harness is the code around the model:

17
00:01:01,807 --> 00:01:04,873
the loop, its limits,
its context and its checks.

18
00:01:05,193 --> 00:01:08,670
Today we sort every line of output
into these two columns.

19
00:01:08,917 --> 00:01:12,736
Lecture 7 and the README
share seven sections, in order.

20
00:01:13,056 --> 00:01:16,314
The tutorial runs six acts
in a fictional cheese shop,

21
00:01:16,314 --> 00:01:19,391
where nothing is really bought,
shipped or emailed.

22
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:22,473
The tracker,
top left, shows the README step;

23
00:01:22,473 --> 00:01:25,628
the gray tag,
top right, shows the lecture slides.

24
00:01:25,948 --> 00:01:28,956
When a tag says your turn,
pause and run the command.

25
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:32,797
One honest limit:

26
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:38,432
we recorded this on October 20, 2026,
without an OpenAI key.

27
00:01:38,752 --> 00:01:41,167
Everything offline ran for real;

28
00:01:41,167 --> 00:01:45,621
live commands ran
until OpenAI refused the placeholder key.

29
00:01:45,941 --> 00:01:50,165
Live answers come from the staff's saved
Colab run and the README.

30
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,024
The only cost is OpenAI tokens.

31
00:01:53,344 --> 00:01:58,229
Customer IDs are masked on screen,
and every command is in the study guide.

32
00:02:00,767 --> 00:02:04,991
Your laptop holds the clone,
with an empty secrets folder beside it.

33
00:02:05,311 --> 00:02:07,278
One container mounts both,

34
00:02:07,278 --> 00:02:12,272
your key goes in as an environment variable,
no port is published,

35
00:02:12,272 --> 00:02:15,828
and the app's only call out
is HTTPS to OpenAI.

36
00:02:16,067 --> 00:02:20,125
Two extras first:
git clone, since the README assumes a copy,

37
00:02:20,125 --> 00:02:25,048
then change into the agent-harness folder,
where every setup command runs.

38
00:02:25,368 --> 00:02:28,760
A listing shows no docs folder;
that matters later.

39
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,093
Step 1 makes the secrets folder,
one level up.

40
00:02:32,413 --> 00:02:35,293
Nothing reads it today,
so no service account.

41
00:02:35,613 --> 00:02:36,766
Pause and run it.

42
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,728
Step 2 exports your key.

43
00:02:41,048 --> 00:02:44,952
Replace the placeholder with your own,
or skip this line offline.

44
00:02:45,272 --> 00:02:48,515
We typed it as written;
act 1 shows what that does.

45
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:53,913
Step 3, bash docker-shell.sh, builds
the image and starts the container.

46
00:02:54,233 --> 00:02:58,695
Ours took 38 seconds,
with the base image already on the machine;

47
00:02:58,695 --> 00:03:02,745
your first build also pulls it,
so expect a minute or more.

48
00:03:02,984 --> 00:03:07,218
The Dockerfile installs from the lockfile
before it copies the code:

49
00:03:07,218 --> 00:03:09,149
the layer trick from lecture 2.

50
00:03:09,384 --> 00:03:12,037
The banner says the environment is activated

51
00:03:12,037 --> 00:03:14,931
before the script activates it:
a harmless slip.

52
00:03:15,251 --> 00:03:18,002
After the at sign comes the container ID.

53
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:21,685
From here on,
every command runs inside the container.

54
00:03:22,005 --> 00:03:25,376
Pause, run steps 2 and 3,
and wait for this prompt.

55
00:03:27,617 --> 00:03:29,708
Three extras from a second terminal.

56
00:03:30,028 --> 00:03:31,969
docker ps: no ports.

57
00:03:32,289 --> 00:03:36,514
docker images:
1.77 gigabytes, since the framework

58
00:03:36,514 --> 00:03:38,796
installs every integration.

59
00:03:39,034 --> 00:03:43,137
docker inspect shows your key
in the container's configuration;

60
00:03:43,137 --> 00:03:45,092
treat a real key as sensitive.

61
00:03:45,334 --> 00:03:49,570
Step 4 is for a container already running
when you changed the key:

62
00:03:49,570 --> 00:03:54,059
the script then only runs docker exec,
which doesn't bring the new key.

63
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:57,099
If that's you,
shell in from a second terminal

64
00:03:57,099 --> 00:03:59,194
and type the README's three lines:

65
00:03:59,194 --> 00:04:02,151
the key goes in hidden,
and out of your history.

66
00:04:02,471 --> 00:04:05,202
Keep working in that shell. Pause here.

67
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,444
Now verify, offline, inside the container.

68
00:04:12,764 --> 00:04:18,115
Step 1 checks the SDK
and prints the installed versions,

69
00:04:18,115 --> 00:04:20,504
at the time of recording:

70
00:04:20,504 --> 00:04:26,524
Python 3.13.16, agent-framework 1.19.0,
and every feature true.

71
00:04:26,767 --> 00:04:33,551
Step 2 runs the tests: 281 of them,
in about 100 seconds, ending in OK.

72
00:04:33,871 --> 00:04:38,500
They use scripted model responses,
so they test the machinery, not the model.

73
00:04:39,750 --> 00:04:44,772
Step 3 shows the logs: deliberate failures,
like an injected audit outage,

74
00:04:44,772 --> 00:04:48,369
and an approval-identity warning
we'll meet in act 4.

75
00:04:48,689 --> 00:04:50,075
Trust the last line.

76
00:04:50,317 --> 00:04:54,430
The model check prints the default
without calling it:

77
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:57,400
gpt-5.6-luna, at the time of recording.

78
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:00,506
A --model flag beats
an environment variable,

79
00:05:00,506 --> 00:05:04,653
which beats this default;
if your account can't use it, set either.

80
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:08,207
The README's notebook section
has two more test commands.

81
00:05:08,527 --> 00:05:10,755
They run in the container too:

82
00:05:10,755 --> 00:05:14,393
9 notebook tests and 12 chat view tests,
both OK.

83
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:17,711
There's a second road: the course Colab.

84
00:05:18,031 --> 00:05:23,521
Save a copy to your Drive, add your key
as a secret named OPENAI_API_KEY,

85
00:05:23,521 --> 00:05:27,055
allow notebook access,
and run the setup cells.

86
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:32,049
The Colab setup installs
the seven pinned packages, not the lockfile,

87
00:05:32,049 --> 00:05:36,729
so their dependencies can drift;
for identical versions, use Docker.

88
00:05:36,967 --> 00:05:41,255
Every recorded answer in this video comes
from this notebook's saved run.

89
00:05:41,575 --> 00:05:44,135
Pause, and verify your setup either way.

90
00:05:47,283 --> 00:05:49,853
Act 1 starts from the lecture's recap:

91
00:05:49,853 --> 00:05:53,140
an agent is a model
with instructions and tools,

92
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:55,262
running until the task is done.

93
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:59,586
In code, the function build_agent
makes one framework agent:

94
00:05:59,586 --> 00:06:04,012
the shop instructions, five tools,
and a structured reply format.

95
00:06:04,250 --> 00:06:07,621
The model can only ask for a tool;
the harness runs it.

96
00:06:07,941 --> 00:06:10,608
And none of these five can place an order:

97
00:06:10,608 --> 00:06:14,101
the preview_order tool records
an unvalidated proposal,

98
00:06:14,101 --> 00:06:16,196
with "order_placed" set to False.

99
00:06:16,433 --> 00:06:22,556
Step 1:
python cli.py --act 1 --scenario standard.

100
00:06:22,876 --> 00:06:25,609
You see the ask, call 1 and five tools;

101
00:06:25,609 --> 00:06:29,532
then the call fails with error 401:
the placeholder key.

102
00:06:29,852 --> 00:06:33,734
Fix it in this shell
with the three read lines from setup step 4.

103
00:06:34,054 --> 00:06:36,102
The failed run is still recorded.

104
00:06:36,350 --> 00:06:39,294
Here's what a key gets you,
from the staff's saved run.

105
00:06:39,614 --> 00:06:41,598
The model asks for the catalog;

106
00:06:41,598 --> 00:06:44,781
the harness runs it,
and the result goes back in:

107
00:06:44,781 --> 00:06:45,950
two more messages.

108
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:48,227
Every round splits at the same seam.

109
00:06:48,547 --> 00:06:53,539
The model's plan ends at Requested tools;
the harness executes at Tool call.

110
00:06:53,859 --> 00:06:57,357
The trace shows requests and results,
not hidden reasoning.

111
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:00,203
Then stock, price, pairings and preview.

112
00:07:00,523 --> 00:07:04,232
On call 6 the model answers
without asking for a tool,

113
00:07:04,232 --> 00:07:05,536
and the loop stops.

114
00:07:05,856 --> 00:07:12,942
Messages grew from 1 to 11,
input from 1,942 to 4,540 tokens:

115
00:07:12,942 --> 00:07:18,634
about 21,000 billed input tokens,
for one answer.

116
00:07:18,883 --> 00:07:24,082
Step 2 adds --show-json,
to see the exact message: a short note,

117
00:07:24,082 --> 00:07:29,443
plus the confirmed request as JSON,
where null means not provided.

118
00:07:29,763 --> 00:07:31,811
Pause and run both, with your key.

119
00:07:34,050 --> 00:07:35,991
The input grows with every call.

120
00:07:36,311 --> 00:07:40,236
That's the lecture's question:
how do we manage what goes into the model?

121
00:07:42,183 --> 00:07:46,392
The lecture says it plainly:
if you've written a loop that calls a model

122
00:07:46,392 --> 00:07:49,372
and runs its tool calls,
you're a harness engineer.

123
00:07:49,692 --> 00:07:51,420
And a bare loop has no stop.

124
00:07:51,666 --> 00:07:55,031
Step 3 is a notebook cell,
for Colab or Jupyter,

125
00:07:55,031 --> 00:07:57,554
that wraps build_agent in a harness.

126
00:07:57,874 --> 00:08:01,121
It adds Trace, Budget with 8 model calls,

127
00:08:01,121 --> 00:08:06,109
20 tool calls and 120 seconds,
Context("basic"), and CartCheck.

128
00:08:06,429 --> 00:08:08,477
The CLI builds exactly this.

129
00:08:09,916 --> 00:08:11,761
The inner ring never comes off:

130
00:08:11,761 --> 00:08:14,737
call admission,
the tool audit and the shop rules.

131
00:08:15,057 --> 00:08:18,578
Four harness layers slide on around it,
one feature each.

132
00:08:20,316 --> 00:08:21,831
Budget is a turnstile.

133
00:08:22,151 --> 00:08:24,857
Each model call is admitted and counted;

134
00:08:24,857 --> 00:08:28,421
a ninth fails
with Maximum of 8 model calls reached,

135
00:08:28,421 --> 00:08:30,066
and the lock stays shut.

136
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:34,174
Without Budget,
only the framework's fallback is left:

137
00:08:34,174 --> 00:08:38,406
40 loop iterations, no call limits,
no overall time budget.

138
00:08:39,350 --> 00:08:41,889
CartCheck is the lecture's verify step.

139
00:08:42,209 --> 00:08:44,555
Back to the hook: it checked the items,

140
00:08:44,555 --> 00:08:47,563
not the prose,
so the garbled budget still passed.

141
00:08:47,883 --> 00:08:49,952
It reports, and never repairs.

142
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:53,858
Now the two columns become
the lecture's action boundary:

143
00:08:53,858 --> 00:08:58,477
five concerns, the model's part on the left,
the harness's on the right.

144
00:08:58,716 --> 00:09:00,465
Act 1 fills three rows.

145
00:09:00,785 --> 00:09:04,412
Move right when stakes are high,
or an action can't be undone.

146
00:09:04,650 --> 00:09:05,994
Something to ponder.

147
00:09:06,314 --> 00:09:09,258
Take harness.without("trace"),
and run that copy.

148
00:09:09,578 --> 00:09:11,903
Is a tool call still counted and audited?

149
00:09:12,223 --> 00:09:14,548
And does the original harness still trace?

150
00:09:23,383 --> 00:09:24,578
Yes, and yes.

151
00:09:24,898 --> 00:09:27,287
Admission and the audit are the inner ring.

152
00:09:27,607 --> 00:09:31,462
And the .add() and .without()
methods never change a harness:

153
00:09:31,462 --> 00:09:36,012
each returns a new configuration,
so the original keeps all four layers.

154
00:09:36,250 --> 00:09:39,045
Step 4 runs the missing-details scenario.

155
00:09:39,365 --> 00:09:43,266
With a key, look for a question back,
not invented details;

156
00:09:43,266 --> 00:09:45,381
CartCheck should report no_cart.

157
00:09:45,701 --> 00:09:46,852
Pause and run it.

158
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,203
Act 2 changes what the model sees:
the lecture's invisible layer.

159
00:09:54,523 --> 00:09:57,678
Here is one model call
as the lecture's stack:

160
00:09:57,678 --> 00:10:02,821
system prompt and shop policies,
the request, the reply format, five tools,

161
00:10:02,821 --> 00:10:06,661
and two slots,
for retrieved data and saved preferences.

162
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:09,003
Basic mode, the one act 1 used,

163
00:10:09,003 --> 00:10:12,307
sends the first four
and leaves the slots empty;

164
00:10:12,307 --> 00:10:13,684
enriched fills them.

165
00:10:15,433 --> 00:10:19,269
Step 1,
--compare, uses the personalized scenario,

166
00:10:19,269 --> 00:10:24,409
where today's request leaves out the second
customer's saved taste:

167
00:10:24,409 --> 00:10:27,401
mild cheeses and nonalcoholic pairings.

168
00:10:27,721 --> 00:10:32,372
The basic run starts and stops at the 401,
so enriched never starts.

169
00:10:32,617 --> 00:10:36,357
With a key, it prints
a side-by-side table and a verdict,

170
00:10:36,357 --> 00:10:38,654
which may be a tie or a regression.

171
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:41,631
Steps 2 to 4 are optional single runs.

172
00:10:41,951 --> 00:10:45,171
Even without a key,
there's a real difference:

173
00:10:45,171 --> 00:10:48,881
basic sends 1 conversation message,
enriched sends 2;

174
00:10:48,881 --> 00:10:51,401
the extra one is the context packet.

175
00:10:51,867 --> 00:10:55,376
Step 4, the override run,
asks for funk 4 or more,

176
00:10:55,376 --> 00:10:58,885
and today's request must win
over the saved taste.

177
00:10:59,133 --> 00:11:03,762
The README says each act 2 command
also prints a harness policy check.

178
00:11:04,082 --> 00:11:06,792
A small slip: --compare doesn't.

179
00:11:07,112 --> 00:11:08,528
This extra call does:

180
00:11:08,528 --> 00:11:11,561
the same raw-milk cart
fails for Pennsylvania

181
00:11:11,561 --> 00:11:14,749
and passes for New York,
under a fictional rule.

182
00:11:18,233 --> 00:11:21,733
Step 5 is an act 3 command
the README puts here;

183
00:11:21,733 --> 00:11:23,119
part 2 explains it.

184
00:11:23,967 --> 00:11:29,364
Step 6 repeats both cases in both modes,
3 times: 12 live runs.

185
00:11:29,684 --> 00:11:34,733
Without a key it stops after 3 errors,
but the report still writes in step 7,

186
00:11:34,733 --> 00:11:38,537
and the label is used up:
with your key, choose a new one.

187
00:11:38,783 --> 00:11:42,699
The page linked here belongs
in the docs folder we saw missing,

188
00:11:42,699 --> 00:11:44,564
so use the README checkpoints.

189
00:11:44,884 --> 00:11:46,485
Pause here if you have a key.

190
00:11:49,833 --> 00:11:53,908
The README prints Inspect the context
first after the live runs.

191
00:11:54,228 --> 00:11:58,366
Without a key, start here:
--preview-context, offline.

192
00:11:58,686 --> 00:12:01,794
Both modes share
instructions, message and tools;

193
00:12:01,794 --> 00:12:03,316
only the packets differ.

194
00:12:03,550 --> 00:12:05,278
Basic has no sources.

195
00:12:05,598 --> 00:12:08,471
Enriched has three, each with a trust label:

196
00:12:08,471 --> 00:12:12,126
the confirmed request, shop data,
and saved preferences.

197
00:12:12,366 --> 00:12:15,086
Twelve cheeses fall through three sieves:

198
00:12:15,086 --> 00:12:19,065
enough stock, no raw milk to Pennsylvania,
no nut allergens.

199
00:12:19,385 --> 00:12:23,138
Munster has no stock, Brie and Comté
fail the shipping rule,

200
00:12:23,138 --> 00:12:25,827
and two nut cheeses fail the allergy check.

201
00:12:26,147 --> 00:12:27,321
Seven survive.

202
00:12:31,266 --> 00:12:34,960
The lecture's retrieved information
is any outside data;

203
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:39,181
here it's a Python filter,
not the vector search from lecture 5.

204
00:12:39,416 --> 00:12:40,632
And where does it go?

205
00:12:40,952 --> 00:12:44,624
Into a user message headed
Retrieved context, data,

206
00:12:44,624 --> 00:12:48,440
not instructions,
so it cannot pose as a system rule.

207
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:50,722
That's the lecture's two lanes:

208
00:12:50,722 --> 00:12:54,456
instructions in the system lane,
evidence in the user lane.

209
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:56,044
Something to ponder.

210
00:12:56,364 --> 00:12:59,116
The enriched packet already removed Comté.

211
00:12:59,436 --> 00:13:01,847
Can the model still put Comté in its cart?

212
00:13:02,167 --> 00:13:04,321
And does anything in act 2 stop it?

213
00:13:13,166 --> 00:13:16,768
Yes: the get_catalog tool
still returns all twelve,

214
00:13:16,768 --> 00:13:19,310
and in act 2 CartCheck only reports.

215
00:13:19,630 --> 00:13:24,644
Selection narrows, but validation decides:
the act 3 workflow blocks it.

216
00:13:24,883 --> 00:13:28,254
Step 9 is the optional baseline,
in both modes.

217
00:13:28,574 --> 00:13:32,002
For standard,
the saved preferences repeat the request,

218
00:13:32,002 --> 00:13:33,310
so little may change.

219
00:13:33,630 --> 00:13:35,656
Pause and run steps 8 and 9.

220
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:41,641
So selecting candidates helps,
but validation decides,

221
00:13:41,641 --> 00:13:44,897
and today's request outranks
saved preferences.

222
00:13:45,217 --> 00:13:49,292
In part 2, the harness takes
the cart's route out of the model's hands.

223
00:13:51,233 --> 00:13:53,709
Part 2 picks up at the container prompt;

224
00:13:53,709 --> 00:13:56,865
if you closed it,
rerun the setup and set your key.

225
00:13:57,185 --> 00:13:59,153
Remember the move-right arrow:

226
00:13:59,153 --> 00:14:02,170
when stakes are high,
actions can't be undone,

227
00:14:02,170 --> 00:14:06,572
the task is predictable,
or you need an audit, give control to code.

228
00:14:06,816 --> 00:14:08,672
Act 3 does exactly that.

229
00:14:08,992 --> 00:14:12,277
The order route is a graph,
and Python owns its edges.

230
00:14:12,597 --> 00:14:17,805
Confirm, propose, validate, and back
to propose only if the report fails,

231
00:14:17,805 --> 00:14:23,584
at most twice; then pairings, manager review
when required, revalidate and place.

232
00:14:23,904 --> 00:14:28,235
Only one node belongs to the model:
propose cart, with no tools.

233
00:14:28,483 --> 00:14:33,553
The README files this under the harness;
on the lecture's autonomy spectrum,

234
00:14:33,553 --> 00:14:37,955
it sits near structured output:
a fixed graph with one model step.

235
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:40,952
Step 1: act 3, scenario standard.

236
00:14:41,272 --> 00:14:44,121
With a key, the model proposes quantities;

237
00:14:44,121 --> 00:14:48,461
code does the rest:
transitions, pricing, validation, placement.

238
00:14:48,781 --> 00:14:50,296
Success is a receipt.

239
00:14:50,533 --> 00:14:55,210
Steps 2 and 3 are the shipping lesson,
with a customer's own cart:

240
00:14:55,210 --> 00:14:58,469
Comté, Bûcheron and Époisses,
to Pennsylvania.

241
00:14:58,789 --> 00:15:03,312
Before, the model only records the cart;
the harness checks it and stops.

242
00:15:03,632 --> 00:15:07,557
After, the revise loop opens,
and the live model may fix it.

243
00:15:08,216 --> 00:15:11,645
As an extra,
--fixture runs the same path offline,

244
00:15:11,645 --> 00:15:13,976
with a script in the model's seat.

245
00:15:14,296 --> 00:15:18,917
Attempt 1 has raw-milk Comté,
and the rail sends it back;

246
00:15:18,917 --> 00:15:22,403
attempt 2 swaps in Taleggio: valid, $50.10.

247
00:15:24,466 --> 00:15:26,115
One trap: with fixtures,

248
00:15:26,115 --> 00:15:31,564
the blocked scenario proposes the same cart
three times and ends UNRESOLVED,

249
00:15:31,564 --> 00:15:35,794
not BLOCKED, so in Colab keep
fixtures off for this lesson.

250
00:15:36,114 --> 00:15:38,184
Pause and run steps 1 to 3.

251
00:15:41,533 --> 00:15:45,828
Step 4 is scripted,
so approval is predictable:

252
00:15:45,828 --> 00:15:50,182
60 guests in New York,
a valid cart at $220.80,

253
00:15:50,182 --> 00:15:52,498
above the $200 threshold.

254
00:15:52,733 --> 00:15:55,577
The workflow pauses,
and you are the manager:

255
00:15:55,577 --> 00:15:57,725
the host answers, never the agent.

256
00:15:58,045 --> 00:15:59,112
Type approve.

257
00:15:59,350 --> 00:16:00,566
What did you approve?

258
00:16:00,886 --> 00:16:04,832
A fingerprint: the SHA-256 hash,
taken over the request,

259
00:16:04,832 --> 00:16:09,270
the items, the subtotal, the policy
and the product attributes.

260
00:16:09,590 --> 00:16:13,472
Stock is left out on purpose:
it's rechecked, not approved.

261
00:16:13,716 --> 00:16:16,895
At checkout the cart is validated
and hashed again.

262
00:16:17,215 --> 00:16:20,362
The approval counts
only if the decision is approve,

263
00:16:20,362 --> 00:16:23,871
and the ticket matches this checkout
and this fingerprint.

264
00:16:24,191 --> 00:16:26,644
Change one line, and the hashes differ.

265
00:16:28,550 --> 00:16:31,472
Your thinking time doesn't count
against the budget.

266
00:16:32,150 --> 00:16:36,843
And as an extra, type decline instead:
the workflow ends, with no order.

267
00:16:37,083 --> 00:16:38,427
Something to ponder.

268
00:16:38,747 --> 00:16:40,176
The manager approves.

269
00:16:40,496 --> 00:16:44,358
Before checkout runs,
another order takes most of the Mimolette.

270
00:16:44,678 --> 00:16:48,667
What does checkout do, and why
doesn't the approval protect this order?

271
00:16:57,516 --> 00:17:01,249
Checkout revalidates,
finds a stock violation, and blocks.

272
00:17:01,569 --> 00:17:05,928
Stock isn't in the fingerprint:
an approval authorizes a decision;

273
00:17:05,928 --> 00:17:07,777
it doesn't reserve anything.

274
00:17:08,097 --> 00:17:09,868
Act 6 shows this for real.

275
00:17:10,116 --> 00:17:14,084
Step 5: Munster has 0 grams,
so attempt 1 fails.

276
00:17:14,404 --> 00:17:17,428
One bounded revision swaps in Époisses,

277
00:17:17,428 --> 00:17:21,615
and the order places at $50.10,
with zero model calls.

278
00:17:21,850 --> 00:17:23,791
Step 6 is the notebook cell.

279
00:17:24,111 --> 00:17:26,857
Its trace and budget layers can come off;

280
00:17:26,857 --> 00:17:32,282
validation, approval and revalidation
are graph and checkout rules, so they stay.

281
00:17:32,516 --> 00:17:37,508
So an approval binds this exact cart,
and checkout revalidates after the pause.

282
00:17:37,828 --> 00:17:41,583
Pause and run steps 4 and 5;
the cell is for Colab.

283
00:17:44,933 --> 00:17:49,648
Act 4 is a planner for a longer task:
a 40-guest event in New York.

284
00:17:49,968 --> 00:17:53,767
The lecture splits memory into long-term
and short-term;

285
00:17:53,767 --> 00:17:56,277
here that's saved memory and history,

286
00:17:56,277 --> 00:18:00,144
plus tasks, approvals and the audit:
five kinds of state.

287
00:18:00,383 --> 00:18:03,498
The README asks
which of these can authorize an action.

288
00:18:03,818 --> 00:18:05,866
Only one: the approval drawer.

289
00:18:06,100 --> 00:18:07,721
Step 1: act 4.

290
00:18:08,041 --> 00:18:12,500
Before any model call, the harness
prints the first customer's saved memory.

291
00:18:12,820 --> 00:18:15,557
Then the planner's tools: assess the event,

292
00:18:15,557 --> 00:18:19,168
read the vendor document,
draft and save a vendor email,

293
00:18:19,168 --> 00:18:20,457
and the to-do tools.

294
00:18:20,777 --> 00:18:22,398
No tool writes memory.

295
00:18:23,266 --> 00:18:25,869
With a key, look for the brief:

296
00:18:25,869 --> 00:18:31,159
1,000 grams each of Comté and Mimolette,
and 1,200 of Époisses.

297
00:18:31,479 --> 00:18:36,066
The shop has only 900 grams of Époisses:
300 short.

298
00:18:36,386 --> 00:18:41,186
A finished task is not an approval,
and shop stock is not vendor availability.

299
00:18:41,433 --> 00:18:44,784
Saving the email
has approval_mode="always_require",

300
00:18:44,784 --> 00:18:47,684
so the framework pauses
before the save runs.

301
00:18:48,004 --> 00:18:51,502
Act 3 paused a whole workflow;
this pauses one tool.

302
00:18:51,750 --> 00:18:55,441
The output after you approve comes
from a scripted extra run.

303
00:18:55,761 --> 00:18:57,992
The review shows the vendor address

304
00:18:57,992 --> 00:19:01,115
and a body written by code,
nut allergy included.

305
00:19:01,435 --> 00:19:05,403
Then: status: saved, email transmitted:
no, and the file path.

306
00:19:05,650 --> 00:19:09,453
Then comes the approval-identity warning
you saw in the tests:

307
00:19:09,453 --> 00:19:12,029
the README predicts it, and it's harmless.

308
00:19:12,349 --> 00:19:14,098
Trust the authoritative block.

309
00:19:14,418 --> 00:19:16,018
Pause and run step 1.

310
00:19:19,367 --> 00:19:23,307
Step 2 runs offline:
a scripted planner with compaction,

311
00:19:23,307 --> 00:19:26,684
a malicious vendor document,
and a test decline.

312
00:19:27,004 --> 00:19:31,314
It first seeds twelve synthetic notes
of about 15,000 characters.

313
00:19:31,550 --> 00:19:37,054
The window is 16,000 tokens,
with 2,400 kept for the reply.

314
00:19:37,374 --> 00:19:42,278
Above 80 percent of the rest,
truncation drops the oldest messages

315
00:19:42,278 --> 00:19:45,993
until the history is back under half:
26 become 5.

316
00:19:46,234 --> 00:19:47,578
Something to ponder.

317
00:19:47,898 --> 00:19:50,550
Compaction dropped 21 of 26 messages,

318
00:19:50,550 --> 00:19:54,063
and none of the history
mentions the nut allergy.

319
00:19:54,383 --> 00:19:56,580
How does every call still know about it?

320
00:20:05,417 --> 00:20:06,420
A capsule.

321
00:20:06,740 --> 00:20:10,683
Before every call,
the harness takes the old one out, compacts,

322
00:20:10,683 --> 00:20:13,438
and pins a fresh one from application state.

323
00:20:13,758 --> 00:20:18,183
It holds the confirmed request
with its nut allergy, the menu,

324
00:20:18,183 --> 00:20:23,252
tasks, pending reviews and open work,
marked as data, not instructions.

325
00:20:24,650 --> 00:20:28,704
That's the lecture's compaction trigger,
and what persists where, in code.

326
00:20:28,950 --> 00:20:31,889
The vendor document says
to ignore the allergy,

327
00:20:31,889 --> 00:20:33,515
so it lands in quarantine.

328
00:20:33,835 --> 00:20:36,013
The detector is deliberately small;

329
00:20:36,013 --> 00:20:39,809
the real guards are the recipient allowlist,
and your review.

330
00:20:40,650 --> 00:20:42,698
The drafted email still says nuts.

331
00:20:43,018 --> 00:20:49,141
The result: DECLINED, no HTML saved,
7 model calls and 7 compactions.

332
00:20:49,384 --> 00:20:53,123
Steps 3 and 4
are the optional memory exercise;

333
00:20:53,123 --> 00:20:57,896
type them from README line 484,
since the ID is masked here.

334
00:20:58,216 --> 00:21:01,879
The first command stores a preference
for the second customer,

335
00:21:01,879 --> 00:21:05,661
and the memory prints before any model call,
even without a key.

336
00:21:05,981 --> 00:21:09,714
The next visit shows it again;
an extra check reads the file.

337
00:21:11,567 --> 00:21:14,852
The lecture lets the model
suggest what's worth remembering.

338
00:21:15,172 --> 00:21:19,652
This harness moves even that to the right:
only the host writes memory.

339
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:25,641
Step 5 is the notebook cell, for Colab:
build_act4 adds five layers.

340
00:21:25,961 --> 00:21:28,927
Without compaction,
the capsule is still re-pinned.

341
00:21:29,247 --> 00:21:31,274
Pause and run steps 2 to 4.

342
00:21:34,617 --> 00:21:35,914
Act 5 adds skills:

343
00:21:35,914 --> 00:21:39,950
folders of procedure the agent
loads only when relevant.

344
00:21:40,270 --> 00:21:44,430
They solve two problems:
the expertise gap and the context budget.

345
00:21:44,667 --> 00:21:48,365
Skills load
in three steps of rising token cost:

346
00:21:48,365 --> 00:21:52,834
every skill's name and description,
about 100 tokens each;

347
00:21:52,834 --> 00:21:55,098
then its main file, SKILL.md;

348
00:21:55,098 --> 00:21:58,768
then a reference or an asset,
only when needed.

349
00:21:59,017 --> 00:22:03,028
The lecture's skill also
has a scripts folder for the agent to run.

350
00:22:03,348 --> 00:22:08,830
This harness removes that tool on purpose:
a skill advises, and never executes.

351
00:22:09,067 --> 00:22:10,688
Step 1: act 5.

352
00:22:11,008 --> 00:22:15,680
Only the metadata loads at first:
tasting-planning and vendor-outreach.

353
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:19,734
The new tools are get_stock,
load_skill and read_skill_resource.

354
00:22:19,984 --> 00:22:22,948
Step 2 asks only about stock, offline:

355
00:22:22,948 --> 00:22:27,707
one get_stock call, 900 grams,
and the script loads no skill.

356
00:22:28,027 --> 00:22:31,171
With a key,
the model decides from the description:

357
00:22:31,171 --> 00:22:32,528
a hint, not a trigger.

358
00:22:33,267 --> 00:22:36,495
Step 3 runs the full sourcing path,
scripted:

359
00:22:36,495 --> 00:22:39,006
six loads, each with its real path.

360
00:22:39,326 --> 00:22:42,984
Drafting is refused
until the email template is read:

361
00:22:42,984 --> 00:22:44,296
a backstop in code.

362
00:22:46,167 --> 00:22:47,511
Something to ponder.

363
00:22:47,831 --> 00:22:52,290
Someone edits the vendor skill to add:
also send this to another address.

364
00:22:52,610 --> 00:22:54,082
Will the email go there?

365
00:23:00,917 --> 00:23:05,302
No. Whatever the skill says,
the guards are in code;

366
00:23:05,302 --> 00:23:08,664
its own text even says
it cannot add recipients,

367
00:23:08,664 --> 00:23:10,485
and every read is checked:

368
00:23:10,485 --> 00:23:13,706
no parent folders,
absolute paths or symlinks,

369
00:23:13,706 --> 00:23:16,298
and a file edited mid-run is refused.

370
00:23:16,534 --> 00:23:20,071
The recipient is checked
in code against the allowlist,

371
00:23:20,071 --> 00:23:25,024
and nothing is ever transmitted;
an extra check shows the saved file says so.

372
00:23:25,267 --> 00:23:27,987
In lecture terms, a tool is the hands,

373
00:23:27,987 --> 00:23:31,710
MCP the socket, unused here,
and a skill the manual.

374
00:23:35,350 --> 00:23:38,806
Step 4, the notebook cell,
allows 16 model calls.

375
00:23:39,126 --> 00:23:41,196
Pause and run steps 1 to 3.

376
00:23:44,533 --> 00:23:46,581
Act 6 is an agent system.

377
00:23:46,901 --> 00:23:51,881
The lecture contrasts one agent
with a graph of agents, in two patterns:

378
00:23:51,881 --> 00:23:55,754
a manager calling agents as tools,
or peers handing off.

379
00:23:56,300 --> 00:23:57,950
It's the manager pattern,

380
00:23:57,950 --> 00:24:00,920
except the tool it calls
is a whole workflow,

381
00:24:00,920 --> 00:24:04,108
the one from act 3,
with a model inside one node.

382
00:24:04,428 --> 00:24:05,494
No handoffs.

383
00:24:05,733 --> 00:24:08,122
Step 1, the notebook cell, builds it.

384
00:24:08,442 --> 00:24:12,363
The budget:
16 outer model calls, 20 tool calls,

385
00:24:12,363 --> 00:24:17,509
8 proposer calls per workflow,
and one shared 180-second clock.

386
00:24:17,750 --> 00:24:21,782
The host confirms the requests;
the model only picks an ID.

387
00:24:22,102 --> 00:24:26,326
Its tools are narrow:
start_order, get_order_status, assess_event.

388
00:24:26,566 --> 00:24:28,657
Step 2: act 6, standard.

389
00:24:28,977 --> 00:24:32,893
With a key, tell outer model calls
apart from workflow calls;

390
00:24:32,893 --> 00:24:35,782
the outer agent
cannot approve its own order.

391
00:24:36,016 --> 00:24:39,579
Step 3 runs offline:
two orders sharing one shop.

392
00:24:39,899 --> 00:24:44,104
Both carts pass at $220.80,
both pause for a manager,

393
00:24:44,104 --> 00:24:46,960
and the test decision approves both.

394
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:50,869
Mimolette and Taleggio start
at 3,000 grams each.

395
00:24:51,189 --> 00:24:54,603
Request 1 is placed, leaving 1,400.

396
00:24:54,923 --> 00:24:59,808
Request 2 reaches revalidation
needing 1,600: blocked.

397
00:25:00,128 --> 00:25:04,586
That's the ponder from act 3, for real:
approval is not placement.

398
00:25:04,833 --> 00:25:08,019
The final answer
is the lecture's response step:

399
00:25:08,019 --> 00:25:10,076
the app checks the model's plan

400
00:25:10,076 --> 00:25:14,860
and fills in quantities, prices
and the status, placed, from the receipt.

401
00:25:15,180 --> 00:25:18,271
The blocked order says Invalid
or unauthorized;

402
00:25:18,271 --> 00:25:20,769
the stock lines below give the reason.

403
00:25:21,016 --> 00:25:24,503
On the autonomy spectrum it's a hybrid,
inside out:

404
00:25:24,503 --> 00:25:27,715
the agent outside,
the fixed graph as its tool.

405
00:25:28,035 --> 00:25:30,062
Pause and run steps 2 and 3.

406
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:37,620
Everything between the provider's model
and the product is ours:

407
00:25:37,620 --> 00:25:39,928
context, harness, tools and skills.

408
00:25:40,248 --> 00:25:42,040
So all of it must be tested.

409
00:25:42,283 --> 00:25:46,336
The lecture measures three things:
outcome, trajectory and cost.

410
00:25:46,656 --> 00:25:50,433
Here they're the outcome and process checks,
plus calls and tokens.

411
00:25:50,883 --> 00:25:53,272
Evaluation step 1 lists the runs.

412
00:25:53,592 --> 00:25:56,744
Every run so far is in one SQLite database,

413
00:25:56,744 --> 00:26:00,483
including the failed live ones, with status:
error.

414
00:26:00,733 --> 00:26:02,906
--inspect-run takes a run ID:

415
00:26:02,906 --> 00:26:06,728
typed literally, it fails,
because it's a template.

416
00:26:07,048 --> 00:26:10,483
Paste a real one;
we use the manager-approval run.

417
00:26:11,333 --> 00:26:15,887
The events read in order:
approval.requested with the fingerprint,

418
00:26:15,887 --> 00:26:17,844
approval.decided by a human,

419
00:26:17,844 --> 00:26:21,338
policy.decision allow,
order.placed, run.finished.

420
00:26:21,658 --> 00:26:25,051
The evidence for order placed is an event,
not the prose.

421
00:26:27,167 --> 00:26:32,052
As an extra, inspect the first failed run:
model.failed, with the 401.

422
00:26:32,372 --> 00:26:34,143
A failure is evidence too.

423
00:26:34,383 --> 00:26:38,885
Evaluation step 2 starts offline:
the act 2 evaluation,

424
00:26:38,885 --> 00:26:42,895
scripted,
two repeats, labeled context-fixture-1.

425
00:26:43,215 --> 00:26:44,344
All 4 runs pass,

426
00:26:44,344 --> 00:26:48,015
but scripted runs test the machinery,
not the model.

427
00:26:48,250 --> 00:26:52,282
Then read the report back,
and export the HTML scorecard,

428
00:26:52,282 --> 00:26:54,970
to its default path or one you choose.

429
00:26:56,550 --> 00:26:59,238
Open it from the outputs
folder on your laptop.

430
00:27:00,567 --> 00:27:04,830
The last command is a template
for a database copied from Colab:

431
00:27:04,830 --> 00:27:06,562
typed literally, it fails.

432
00:27:06,882 --> 00:27:10,274
Labels are immutable:
reuse one, and the batch stops.

433
00:27:10,594 --> 00:27:13,580
Pause and run evaluation steps 1 and 2.

434
00:27:16,917 --> 00:27:20,416
Evaluation
step 3 compares two configurations.

435
00:27:20,736 --> 00:27:21,766
The lecture warns

436
00:27:21,766 --> 00:27:24,917
that a prompt edit can fix one case
and break three,

437
00:27:24,917 --> 00:27:26,112
so compare per case.

438
00:27:26,350 --> 00:27:29,388
The lecture's improvement loop:
see a failure,

439
00:27:29,388 --> 00:27:33,417
capture a test case, change one layer,
run the suite k times,

440
00:27:33,417 --> 00:27:36,654
compare with the baseline,
then merge or iterate.

441
00:27:38,417 --> 00:27:42,918
Batch A runs enriched mode on two cases,
twice: 4 live runs.

442
00:27:43,238 --> 00:27:47,014
Without a key: 0 of 4,
with 3 errors and 1 missing.

443
00:27:47,334 --> 00:27:51,004
If one fails,
fix the key and rerun both with new labels.

444
00:27:51,250 --> 00:27:54,636
Copy the prompt file
and edit the copy on your laptop:

445
00:27:54,636 --> 00:27:58,525
one deliberate change,
keeping the confirmed constraint rules.

446
00:27:58,767 --> 00:28:02,987
Our extra appends a line asking for a price
and an action status;

447
00:28:02,987 --> 00:28:04,676
diff shows just that line.

448
00:28:05,450 --> 00:28:11,274
Batch B runs the same cases and settings,
with --prompt-file pointing at the copy.

449
00:28:11,517 --> 00:28:13,074
Then compare, offline.

450
00:28:13,394 --> 00:28:15,720
Changed configuration: prompt.

451
00:28:16,040 --> 00:28:18,130
Confounding changes: none.

452
00:28:18,450 --> 00:28:21,558
Change code, data or the model,
and it says so:

453
00:28:21,558 --> 00:28:25,064
the lecture's change-one-layer rule,
checked in code.

454
00:28:25,500 --> 00:28:28,989
Compare flags a run
only when pass or fail flips;

455
00:28:28,989 --> 00:28:31,410
here every run errored both times.

456
00:28:32,450 --> 00:28:37,767
As an extra, comparing two scripted batches
reports all four runs unchanged,

457
00:28:37,767 --> 00:28:40,216
because fixtures ignore the prompt.

458
00:28:40,450 --> 00:28:43,629
Evaluation step 4 asks
what the checks establish.

459
00:28:43,949 --> 00:28:47,726
They check properties,
like cart validity and approval order;

460
00:28:47,726 --> 00:28:51,565
the prose needs your review,
with the README's five questions.

461
00:28:51,800 --> 00:28:55,747
The lecture lists three judges:
code, a model and a human.

462
00:28:56,067 --> 00:28:58,819
This tutorial uses two: code, and you.

463
00:28:59,139 --> 00:29:03,064
Lean toward code as far as the task allows,
as in the harness.

464
00:29:03,300 --> 00:29:06,393
Report one difference,
its evidence, and one limit.

465
00:29:06,713 --> 00:29:11,193
And keep your cases:
the eval set is the most valuable artifact.

466
00:29:11,513 --> 00:29:13,924
Pause and run evaluation step 3.

467
00:29:17,267 --> 00:29:22,990
The repository map of section 7 ends
with python cli.py --help:

468
00:29:22,990 --> 00:29:25,352
every option in one place.

469
00:29:25,600 --> 00:29:28,459
Traces and live memory stay in SQLite.

470
00:29:28,779 --> 00:29:33,614
Inventory, orders, tickets
and pending workflows last for one process;

471
00:29:33,614 --> 00:29:36,032
tasks and history, for one session.

472
00:29:36,267 --> 00:29:38,720
Now tear down; these steps are extras.

473
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:41,918
Type exit in each container shell;

474
00:29:41,918 --> 00:29:47,083
it was started with --rm,
so nothing is left in docker ps -a.

475
00:29:47,317 --> 00:29:49,728
The outputs folder stays on your laptop.

476
00:29:50,048 --> 00:29:54,027
Its trace database holds
your prompts and outputs, keys redacted;

477
00:29:54,027 --> 00:29:55,680
delete it when you're done.

478
00:29:55,917 --> 00:30:00,541
docker image rm frees
most of those 1.77 gigabytes;

479
00:30:00,541 --> 00:30:02,445
the base image stays.

480
00:30:02,765 --> 00:30:06,572
Then close the terminal that held your key,
keep it out of files,

481
00:30:06,572 --> 00:30:09,677
commits and your shell history,
and watch your usage.

482
00:30:09,997 --> 00:30:12,088
In Colab, delete the runtime.

483
00:30:12,408 --> 00:30:13,773
Pause, and tear down.

484
00:30:16,017 --> 00:30:20,283
Here is the action boundary, filled in:
budgets and the graph;

485
00:30:20,283 --> 00:30:23,035
schemas, the audit and approval on save;

486
00:30:23,035 --> 00:30:26,338
the packet,
the capsule and host-written memory;

487
00:30:26,338 --> 00:30:30,673
CartCheck and revalidation;
narrow tools and separate counters.

488
00:30:30,917 --> 00:30:32,901
The model proposes, on the left.

489
00:30:33,221 --> 00:30:36,869
On the right, the harness decides,
and where it matters, so do you.

490
00:30:37,117 --> 00:30:39,997
The README ends
with five reflection questions.

491
00:30:40,317 --> 00:30:42,621
Pick one run you did, and answer them.

492
00:30:42,867 --> 00:30:43,976
Test yourself.

493
00:30:44,296 --> 00:30:46,366
What survives harness.without("trace")?

494
00:30:46,686 --> 00:30:49,352
Why does retrieved context arrive as data?

495
00:30:49,672 --> 00:30:51,400
What is an approval bound to?

496
00:30:51,720 --> 00:30:53,683
Which state can authorize an action?

497
00:30:54,003 --> 00:30:56,563
And what does a passing fixture batch
establish?

498
00:30:56,883 --> 00:31:00,339
Pause and try them;
answers are in the exercises.

499
00:31:03,584 --> 00:31:08,252
Next, lecture 8 fine-tunes models
on Google's Agent Platform,

500
00:31:08,252 --> 00:31:09,707
formerly Vertex AI.

501
00:31:10,027 --> 00:31:12,918
Lecture 9 tests code with pytest;

502
00:31:12,918 --> 00:31:17,386
these 281 tests use unittest,
and are a good model.

